# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Description {#description}"
msgstr "Paglalarawan {#description}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Use the LTM AI-QEM function to estimate the expectation values of observables for a given quantum circuit. To use LTM AI-QEM, provide a quantum circuit, a set of observables to measure, and a QPU. The function uses the same QPU time as the IBM Quantum Estimator primitive, and no additional QPU time.\n"
msgstr "Gamitin ang LTM AI-QEM function para tantiyahin ang mga expectation value ng mga observable para sa isang quantum circuit. Para magamit ang LTM AI-QEM, magbigay ng quantum circuit, set ng mga observable na susukatin, at isang QPU. Gumagamit ang function ng parehong QPU time gaya ng IBM Quantum Estimator primitive, at walang karagdagang QPU time.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The usual way to compute the expectation value of an observable $O$ is to decompose the observable into a sum of Pauli operators, $O=\\sum_i{c_i}{P_i}$, and calculate the expectation value of each Pauli operator, $\\langle P_i \\rangle$, individually. The expectation value of the observable, $\\langle O \\rangle$, is then the same linear combination of the Pauli expectation values: ${\\langle O \\rangle}=\\sum_i{c_i}{\\langle P_i \\rangle}$. If a set of Pauli operators commute with each other, the measurements required to compute their expectation values can be done simultaneously. When you run a circuit with the LTM AI-QEM function, the function uses the Estimator primitive to run the circuit on hardware as usual. It follows the same procedure of decomposing the observable into Pauli operators and computing their expectation values individually. Then the GCN-Transformer, a deep learning architecture, predicts a correction factor $\\zeta_i$ for each Pauli operator $P_i$. The function uses this correction factor to correct the noisy expectation value of each Pauli operator: ${\\langle P_i \\rangle}_{mitigated} = \\frac{{\\langle P_i \\rangle}_{noisy}}{\\zeta_i}$. The mitigated expectation value is therefore ${\\langle O \\rangle}_{mitigated} = \\sum_i{c_i}{\\langle {P_i} \\rangle}_{mitigated} = \\sum_i{c_i}{\\frac{{\\langle P_i \\rangle}_{noisy}}{\\zeta_i}}$.\n"
msgstr "Ang karaniwang paraan para kalkulahin ang expectation value ng observable na $O$ ay hatiin ang observable sa kabuuan ng mga Pauli operator, $O=\\sum_i{c_i}{P_i}$, at kalkulahin nang isa-isa ang expectation value ng bawat Pauli operator, $\\langle P_i \\rangle$. Ang expectation value ng observable, $\\langle O \\rangle$, ay ang parehong linear combination ng mga Pauli expectation value: ${\\langle O \\rangle}=\\sum_i{c_i}{\\langle P_i \\rangle}$. Kung ang isang set ng mga Pauli operator ay nagko-commute sa isa't isa, maaaring sabay-sabay na gawin ang mga sukat na kailangan para kalkulahin ang kanilang mga expectation value. Kapag nagpatakbo ka ng circuit gamit ang LTM AI-QEM function, ginagamit ng function ang Estimator primitive para patakbuhin ang circuit sa hardware gaya ng dati. Sinusunod nito ang parehong pamamaraan ng paghahati sa observable sa mga Pauli operator at pagkalkula ng kanilang mga expectation value nang isa-isa. Pagkatapos, ang GCN-Transformer, isang deep learning architecture, ay nagpe-predict ng correction factor na $\\zeta_i$ para sa bawat Pauli operator na $P_i$. Ginagamit ng function ang correction factor na ito para itama ang noisy expectation value ng bawat Pauli operator: ${\\langle P_i \\rangle}_{mitigated} = \\frac{{\\langle P_i \\rangle}_{noisy}}{\\zeta_i}$. Samakatuwid, ang mitigated expectation value ay ${\\langle O \\rangle}_{mitigated} = \\sum_i{c_i}{\\langle {P_i} \\rangle}_{mitigated} = \\sum_i{c_i}{\\frac{{\\langle P_i \\rangle}_{noisy}}{\\zeta_i}}$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The workflow for using the LTM AI-QEM function is as follows:\n"
msgstr "Ang workflow para sa paggamit ng LTM AI-QEM function ay ang sumusunod:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Define your circuits and observables.\n"
msgstr "Tukuyin ang iyong mga circuit at observable.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Run the LTM AI-QEM function by passing a circuit and observable pairs as primitive unified blocs (PUBs).\n"
msgstr "Patakbuhin ang LTM AI-QEM function sa pamamagitan ng pagpasa ng circuit at mga pares ng observable bilang primitive unified blocs (PUBs).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Retrieve the results, which contain the expectation value of the observable with respect to the corresponding quantum circuit in each PUB.\n"
msgstr "Kunin ang mga resulta, na naglalaman ng expectation value ng observable kaugnay ng katumbas na quantum circuit sa bawat PUB.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Many test examples empirically show that the LTM AI-QEM function improves the estimation of observables. The function has the following features:\n"
msgstr "Maraming test example ang empirikal na nagpapakitang pinapabuti ng LTM AI-QEM function ang pagtantiya ng mga observable. Ang function ay may mga sumusunod na feature:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Utility-scale experiments:** Test examples show that AI-QEM performs better than other AI-based methods for circuits with large depth and large width. See the [Benchmarks](#benchmarks) section for results.\n"
msgstr "**Mga utility-scale na eksperimento:** Ipinapakita ng mga test example na mas mahusay ang AI-QEM kaysa sa ibang AI-based na paraan para sa mga circuit na may malaking depth at malaking width. Tingnan ang seksyong [Benchmarks](#benchmarks) para sa mga resulta.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Primitive:** The function supports the Estimator primitive, so you can use it to compute the expectation value of any observable with respect to any quantum circuit.\n"
msgstr "**Primitive:** Sinusuportahan ng function ang Estimator primitive, kaya magagamit mo ito para kalkulahin ang expectation value ng anumang observable kaugnay ng anumang quantum circuit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Primitive unified bloc (PUB):** The LTM AI-QEM function supports all types of PUBs (that is, all circuit-observable configurations) that the Estimator primitive supports.\n"
msgstr "**Primitive unified bloc (PUB):** Sinusuportahan ng LTM AI-QEM function ang lahat ng uri ng PUB (ibig sabihin, lahat ng configuration ng circuit-observable) na sinusuportahan ng Estimator primitive.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The AI-QEM technique begins with dataset construction, in which random quantum circuits are generated with various parameter settings. For each circuit, both the ideal expectation values (obtained through simulation) and the noisy expectation values (obtained from hardware execution) are computed. These results, along with the corresponding quantum circuits and backend information, are stored in a dataset repository. The dataset is then used to train a machine-learning or deep-learning model that can mitigate noise in quantum computations, and the trained model is saved for future use. During inference, the trained model takes as input a quantum circuit, its noisy expectation value computed on hardware, the relevant observables, and backend information. The model then processes these inputs to produce a noise-mitigated expectation value.\n"
msgstr "Nagsisimula ang AI-QEM technique sa pagbuo ng dataset, kung saan gumagawa ng mga random na quantum circuit na may iba't ibang setting ng parameter. Para sa bawat circuit, kinakalkula ang parehong ideal na expectation value (nakuha sa pamamagitan ng simulation) at noisy na expectation value (nakuha mula sa pagpapatakbo sa hardware). Ang mga resultang ito, kasama ang kaukulang mga quantum circuit at impormasyon ng backend, ay iniimbak sa isang dataset repository. Ginagamit ang dataset para sanayin ang machine-learning o deep-learning model na kayang bawasan ang noise sa mga quantum computation, at sine-save ang sinanay na model para sa susunod na gamit. Sa inference, ang sinanay na model ay tumatanggap bilang input ng isang quantum circuit, ang noisy expectation value nito na kinalkula sa hardware, ang mga kaugnay na observable, at impormasyon ng backend. Pagkatapos ay pinoproseso ng model ang mga input na ito para makabuo ng expectation value na nabawasan ang noise.\n"

